1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,210
Good morning everyone.

2
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:08,010
So today we're going to look at an important creative activity

3
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:10,380
and that's hand knitting.

4
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:17,827
Ancient knitted garments have been found in many different countries, showing that knitting is

5
00:00:17,827 --> 00:00:21,540
a global activity with a long history.

6
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:28,057
When someone says the word ‘knitting' we might well picture an elderly person –

7
00:00:28,057 --> 00:00:33,852
a grandmother perhaps – sitting by the fire knitting garments for themselves

8
00:00:33,852 --> 00:00:36,880
or other members of the family.

9
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,225
It's a homely image,

10
00:00:39,225 --> 00:00:44,618
but one that may lead you to feel that knitting is an activity of the past –

11
00:00:44,618 --> 00:00:51,233
and, indeed, during the previous decade, it was one of the skills that was

12
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:54,400
predicted to vanish from everyday life.

13
00:00:54,400 --> 00:01:00,689
For although humans have sewn and knitted their own clothing for a very long

14
00:01:00,689 --> 00:01:08,941
time, many of these craft-based skills went into decline when industrial machines took over – mainly

15
00:01:08,941 --> 00:01:15,350
because they were no longer passed down from one generation to another.

16
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:17,074
However,

17
00:01:17,074 --> 00:01:27,259
that's all changing and interest in knitting classes in many countries is actually rising, as more

18
00:01:27,259 --> 00:01:31,530
and more people are seeking formal instruction in the skill.

19
00:01:31,530 --> 00:01:38,476
With that trend, we're also seeing an increase in the sales figures for knitting

20
00:01:38,476 --> 00:01:39,480
equipment.

21
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:45,627
So why do people want to be taught to knit at a time when

22
00:01:45,627 --> 00:01:48,720
a machine can readily do the job for them?

23
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:56,230
The answer is that knitting, as a handicraft, has numerous benefits for those doing

24
00:01:56,230 --> 00:01:56,290
it.

25
00:01:56,290 --> 00:02:00,630
Let's consider what some of these might be.

26
00:02:00,630 --> 00:02:07,168
While many people knitted garments in the past because they couldn't afford to buy

27
00:02:07,168 --> 00:02:14,888
clothes, it's still true today that knitting can be helpful if you're experiencing economic

28
00:02:14,888 --> 00:02:15,570
hardship.

29
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:23,604
If you have several children who all need warm winter clothes, knitting may save

30
00:02:23,604 --> 00:02:25,010
you a lot of money.

31
00:02:25,010 --> 00:02:32,405
And the results of knitting your own clothes can be very rewarding, even though

32
00:02:32,405 --> 00:02:36,894
the skills you need to get going are really quite basic

33
00:02:36,894 --> 00:02:39,880
and the financial outlay is minimal.

34
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:47,300
But the more significant benefits in today's world are to do with well-being.

35
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:52,793
In a world where it's estimated that we spend up to nine hours a

36
00:02:52,793 --> 00:03:00,090
day online, doing something with our hands that is craft-based makes us feel good.

37
00:03:00,090 --> 00:03:10,610
It releases us from the stress of a technological, fast-paced life.

38
00:03:10,610 --> 00:03:16,410
Now, let's look back a bit to early knitting activities.

39
00:03:16,410 --> 00:03:21,662
In fact, no one really knows when knitting first began,

40
00:03:21,662 --> 00:03:29,140
but archaeological remains have disclosed plenty of information for us to think about.

41
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:36,579
One of the interesting things about knitting is that the earliest pieces of clothing

42
00:03:36,579 --> 00:03:43,677
that have been found suggest that most of the items produced were round rather

43
00:03:43,677 --> 00:03:44,780
than flat.

44
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:52,377
Discoveries from the 3rd and 4th centuries in Egypt show that things like socks

45
00:03:52,377 --> 00:03:53,380
and gloves,

46
00:03:53,380 --> 00:04:00,656
that were needed to keep hands and feet warm, were knitted in one piece using four

47
00:04:00,656 --> 00:04:02,180
or five needles.

48
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:06,780
That's very different from most knitting patterns today,

49
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:09,130
which only require two.

50
00:04:09,130 --> 00:04:16,300
What's more, the very first needles people used were hand carved out of wood

51
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:24,591
and other natural materials, like bone, whereas today's needles are largely made of steel

52
00:04:24,591 --> 00:04:31,060
or plastic and make that characteristic clicking sound when someone's using them.

53
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:39,240
Ancient people knitted using yarns made from linen, hemp, cotton and wool,

54
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:43,550
and these were often very rough on the skin.

55
00:04:43,550 --> 00:04:45,094
The spinning wheel,

56
00:04:45,094 --> 00:04:51,191
which allowed people to make finer yarns and produce much greater quantities of them,

57
00:04:51,191 --> 00:04:57,047
led to the dominance of wool in the knitting industry – often favoured for

58
00:04:57,047 --> 00:04:58,290
its warmth.

59
00:04:58,290 --> 00:05:05,008
Another interesting fact about knitting is that because it was practised in

60
00:05:05,008 --> 00:05:12,367
so many parts of the world for so many purposes, regional differences in style

61
00:05:12,367 --> 00:05:13,570
developed.

62
00:05:13,570 --> 00:05:21,210
This visual identity has allowed researchers to match bits of knitted clothing that have

63
00:05:21,210 --> 00:05:26,062
been unearthed over time to the region from which the wearer came

64
00:05:26,062 --> 00:05:29,230
or the job that he or she did.

65
00:05:29,230 --> 00:05:36,752
As I've mentioned, knitting offered people from poor communities a way of making extra

66
00:05:36,752 --> 00:05:39,400
money while doing other tasks.

67
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:47,441
For many centuries, it seems, men, women and children took every opportunity to knit,

68
00:05:47,441 --> 00:05:48,424
for example,

69
00:05:48,424 --> 00:05:54,440
while watching over sheep, walking to market or riding in boats.

70
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:59,000
So, let's move on to take a …
